te Infection: recent cold history could explain leukocytosis, but elevated alkaline phosphatase is atypical for a simple reactive process", "Hyperparathyroidism: elevated calcium (10.9 mEq/L) raises suspicion, but no parathyroid hormone data available to confirm this mechanism", "B2 (danger score 0.8): unspecified but high-risk myeloproliferative alternative with unresolved evidence"], "alarm_features": ["Unintentional weight loss", "Fatigue with systemic involvement", "Elevated white blood cell count with abnormal alkaline phosphatase"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.04116450241549732, "posterior": 0.04116450241549732, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["acute myeloid leukemia", "myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts", "chronic myeloid leukemia in blast crisis"]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.1446994630362936, "posterior": 0.1446994630362936, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["acute lymphoblastic leukemia", "lymphoblastic lymphoma"]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.6640736452274422, "posterior": 0.6640736452274422, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["chronic myeloid leukemia", "polycythemia vera", "essential thrombocythemia", "primary myelofibrosis"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.08731864148741857, "posterior": 0.08731864148741857, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.062370458205298976, "posterior": 0.062370458205298976, "danger": 0.3, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}}, "frontier": ["B1", "B2", "B3", "B4", "B5"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute onset of symptoms over days with constitutional features support a clonal myeloid process over reactive causes?", "summary": "Subacute constitutional symptoms like fatigue and weight loss are more consistent with clonal myeloid processes (e.g., chronic myeloproliferative disorders) than acute reactive causes."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of blasts in the leukocyte differential and presence of constitutional symptoms rule out lymphoid neoplasm with increased blasts?", "summary": "The lack of blasts in the differential makes acute lymphoid neoplasms (B2) less likely, though constitutional symptoms are still compatible with these conditions."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase level argue against chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) in favor of a more chronic myeloproliferative process?", "summary": "Elevated LAPS is atypical for CML (which typically has low LAPS), favoring chronic myeloproliferative disorders like polycythemia vera over CML."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of plasma cell abnormalities or monoclonal protein in lab results argue against multiple myeloma?", "summary": "No plasma cell or monoclonal protein evidence directly excludes multiple myeloma (B4) as a primary consideration."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of infection markers (e.g., neutrophilia with left shift) in the differential support against leukemoid reaction?", "summary": "The normal differential without infection markers makes reactive leukemoid reaction (B5) unlikely despite the high WBC count."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B3", 0.565], ["B2", 0.232], ["B4", 0.098]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No universal instability markers are present. The patient is hemodynamically stable with no signs of airway compromise, respiratory failure, shock, or uncontrolled hemorrhage.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading hypothesis (Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm) has posterior < 0.75 and dangerous alternatives (B2: 0.232, danger 0.8) remain unresolved. Tree depth (1) is below max depth (3)."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.45, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.5646267103719508, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic lymphoma"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic myeloid leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Leukemoid reaction"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'infection', 'neutrophilia'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B3", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.565}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B3", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm", "posterior": 0.664, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2", "B4", "B5"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=60%, source=primekg]\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, decreased circulating antibody level\n  Favours second: abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nRelated to Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: cd4+/cd56+ hematodermic neoplasm, hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, mast cell neoplasm\n\nRelated to Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, lymphoid leukemia (disease), lymphoma\n\nRelated to Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm: bone marrow cancer, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, not otherwise specified\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts"}
